WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.360
Everyone loves the story of Beauty and the
Beast.

00:00:02.750 --> 00:00:06.700
The Disney version of the story was well-received
enough to become the first animated feature

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:08.060
nominated for Best Picture.

00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:13.360
But some people believe the romantic fable
is based in part on a true story - and that

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:14.680
story is pretty dark.

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:21.300
Pedro Gonzales was born in Tenerife in 1537,
a little more than 40 years after Spain conquered

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:22.700
and enslaved the native people.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:27.750
Born with a rare disorder that left him completely
covered in hair, Pedro was given as a gift

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:29.580
to Henry II, the King of France.

00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:34.820
According to The Smithsonian, the French believed
him to be a true beast, and kept him in a

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:36.640
cage, feeding him raw meat and animal feed.

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:41.460
They also changed his name to the fancy Latin
version, Petrus Gonsalvus.

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:46.220
Known today as Ambras syndrome, the condition
Petrus had is incredibly rare.

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:50.840
Thought to affect fewer than one in a billion
people, there have only been about 50 to 100

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:52.500
cases recorded since the Middle Ages.

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:58.000
Though some became famous, such as Shwe-Maong
of Burma, who married into his country's royal

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:02.440
family in the 19th century, many were shunned
and forced to become circus sideshow performers.

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:05.660
And things aren't necessarily much better
today.

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:11.800
In 2011, 12-year-old Supatra Sasuphan was
named "world's hairiest girl" by the Guinness

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:16.300
Book of World Records, a designation which
she noted was a step up from other names that

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:19.100
she's been called, such as "wolf girl" and
"monkey face."

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:24.300
As an experiment, King Henry II took Petrus
out of his cage, dressed him up in the finest

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:28.100
clothes, and began giving him all the same
education the nobles of the period got.

00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:32.900
The boy's fancy education included military
training, etiquette, speech, reading, and

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:33.380
writing.

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:38.300
It turned out he was a prodigy, becoming fluent
in three languages, including Latin.

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:43.540
Petrus became a celebrity, with royal dignitaries
and ambassadors flocking to meet him.

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:47.940
Pleased, Henry promoted him, and Petrus eventually
became a highly ranking member of the King's

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:48.500
attendants.

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:53.230
But he was never truly viewed as an equal
at court, something that became clear when

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:54.380
the King suddenly died.

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:58.820
After Henri II's death, his wife, Catherine
de' Medici, became regent.

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:04.160
She decided to conduct her own cruel experiment,
marrying Petrus off to a servant's beautiful

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:06.540
daughter to see if they could produce more
hairy children.

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:11.820
The bride, who was also named Catherine, reportedly
didn't know who she was marrying until she

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:12.360
arrived at the altar.

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:17.400
Catherine and Petrus ended up having seven
children - and four of them were indeed afflicted

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:18.020
with Ambras syndrome.

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:23.060
The Queen was delighted, as she now had four
more people to show off at parties like trained

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:23.600
pets.

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:28.740
Despite the indignities, there were some perks
to living at court, like a home and an income.

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:31.960
Those were lost when the Queen died in 1589.

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:36.700
Petrus and his family were forced to go on
the road, visiting places like Bologna, Parma,

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:37.920
Ferrara, and Rome.

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:42.320
During their travels, the family was treated
as a roving curiosity by the noble courts

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:42.860
they visited.

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:48.000
They were reportedly well-compensated to entertain
their hosts, but some of the hairy children

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:52.560
were still given away to distant rulers, possibly
to guarantee their safety and financial future.

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:57.340
All of this traveling made the Gonsalvus family
among the most famous individuals in Europe.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:02.190
They were widely studied by the leading scientific
authorities of the day, who treated them with

00:03:02.190 --> 00:03:06.030
far more respect than the general public,
which still viewed the family as little more

00:03:06.030 --> 00:03:07.480
than savages or even animals.

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:12.560
Yet they still became a bit of a cultural
sensation, and no wealthy household was complete

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:16.680
without a painted portrait of Petrus or one
of his famous offspring - which is why so

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:19.040
many images of them still exist today.

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:24.280
According to History vs. Hollywood, after
traveling around Europe for years, the remaining

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:28.760
family members settled in Parma, Italy, where
they were employed by Duke Ranuccio Farnese.

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:33.920
The last mention of Petrus is in 1617, when
he attended the christening of one of his

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:34.460
grandchildren.

00:03:35.140 --> 00:03:39.020
There is no record of his death, but there
is evidence Catherine died in 1623.

00:03:39.530 --> 00:03:43.180
And it's also believed that at least one of
the hairy Gonsalvus daughters married and

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:43.900
had her own children.

00:03:44.680 --> 00:03:48.960
While they were married an estimated 40 years
and had seven children, people still wonder

00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:50.980
if Catherine and Petrus were actually in love.

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:55.100
One hint that they were is in a portrait of
the couple, where she has her hand on his

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:55.660
shoulder.

00:03:56.420 --> 00:04:00.060
It has been noted by historians that this
gesture was used by painters to show genuine

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:04.640
affection between husbands and wives, so it's
possible their relationship truly was a worthy

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:07.240
basis for the love story in Beauty and the
Beast.

